WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:04.237
-Our first guest hosts the Emmy-
and Peabody Award-winning show

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:06.572
"Last Week Tonight,"
which airs Sundays on HBO

00:00:06.572 --> 00:00:07.906
and streams on MAX.

00:00:07.906 --> 00:00:09.108
Please welcome back to the show
our very good friend

00:00:09.108 --> 00:00:10.710
John Oliver, everybody.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.713
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:25.624 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:25.624 --> 00:00:28.227
Yes.

00:00:28.227 --> 00:00:30.863
You know, it was a --
it was a bad time.

00:00:30.863 --> 00:00:34.567
The strike was a bad time.
-Yeah, yeah. Really bad.

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:36.502
-We got to spend some time
together.

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:40.039
-Let's throw - - I know cursing
is --

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:41.875
-Encouraged.
-Oh, sure.

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:43.643
Let's call it a [bleep] bad time

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:46.245
because otherwise,
what are intensifiers for?

00:00:46.245 --> 00:00:47.580
-Yeah.
-Yes. Very bad.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.882
-And, you know, we did
spend some time together

00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:51.050
during the strike.
That was nice.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.618
But we were talking
about a lot of people

00:00:52.618 --> 00:00:55.621
just assume when you're not
working that is a positive.

00:00:55.621 --> 00:00:57.290
-Yeah, they sure do assume
that, don't they?

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:58.558
-Yeah.
-They assume that

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:00.960
almost every day, and they
assume that reminding you

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.363
of that might be a pleasant
thing for you to hear.

00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:05.764
Yeah. It was nonstop.
People would say,

00:01:05.764 --> 00:01:07.066
"Well, at least
you're getting some time off.

00:01:07.066 --> 00:01:08.635
It must feel like a vacation."

00:01:08.635 --> 00:01:13.473
And I most of the time
did not respond to that.

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:15.074
And when I did,
I was not as gracious

00:01:15.074 --> 00:01:17.476
as I might otherwise have been.

00:01:17.476 --> 00:01:19.045
I was on an elevator
with my wife,

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:21.780
and someone got off saying,
"Yeah, well, at least

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.349
you're getting a nice vacation.
That must be nice."

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:28.220
And I was following
them off to say,

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.456
"You're [bleep] wrong
about that,"

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:32.725
until my wife pulled me back,
and said, it's not our floor.

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:35.061
I know it's not our floor.

00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:37.430
But that's -- that's not
appreciating the difficulty

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:40.467
of the situation that
I'm in right now.

00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:42.501
-Right before the strike,
you were here,

00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:43.569
and it was lovely
to have you here,

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:44.937
and your parents
were in the audience.

00:01:44.937 --> 00:01:46.071
-Yes, that's true.

00:01:46.071 --> 00:01:47.974
-Although you stressed to us --
-I did.

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:50.776
-Don't mention it and don't cut
to them.

00:01:50.776 --> 00:01:52.411
-That's right.

00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:54.948
Your producers asked, would they
like a camera on them?

00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:58.417
And I had to say,
I cannot overstate enough

00:01:58.417 --> 00:02:01.321
just how little they want
a camera on them right here.

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:04.090
It would be the meanest thing
I could possibly do to them.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.092
And I've not been
a perfect child.

00:02:07.092 --> 00:02:08.227
But yeah, they really did.

00:02:08.227 --> 00:02:10.096
They were so excited
to be here, though,

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:13.532
and the thing they said
afterwards was it was really,

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:16.803
really fun to go
and watch a real TV show.

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:26.245
And they had been, Seth,
at my show the previous night.

00:02:26.245 --> 00:02:29.548
Gets one step worse because I
went, "Oh, I think I understand

00:02:29.548 --> 00:02:31.084
what you mean,"
because, you know,

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:32.518
it's like it's -- "30 Rock"

00:02:32.518 --> 00:02:35.221
is kind of a really
exciting place to be.

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:36.655
So I think I see it.
And they said, "Well,

00:02:36.655 --> 00:02:39.425
could we go and see Colbert
next time that we're here?"

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:40.626
And I said, "Why?"
And they said,

00:02:40.626 --> 00:02:43.062
"Because it would be nice
to see a real guest."

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:46.398
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.398 --> 00:02:49.101
Wow. Yeah.

00:02:49.101 --> 00:02:52.338
So if there's any sense
of why I'm like this,

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.640
there's a little snapshot.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.809
That's why my feet are --
-You kind of feel like --

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:58.778
there should be
a<i> Huffington Post</i> article

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:03.148
that's like, "John Oliver's
parents destroy John Oliver."

00:03:03.148 --> 00:03:06.385
-Eviscerate their own boy.

00:03:06.385 --> 00:03:08.454
-A very well structured
two-part burn on you.

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:10.122
-Yeah. That's right. Yeah.
-Also that thing of --

00:03:10.122 --> 00:03:12.925
I mean, by the way,
don't blame us for asking like,

00:03:12.925 --> 00:03:14.259
do you want a camera
on your parents?

00:03:14.259 --> 00:03:15.795
Because, you know, of course,
when my parents come,

00:03:15.795 --> 00:03:18.897
they actually bring a camera,

00:03:18.897 --> 00:03:20.633
just in case
something's wrong with ours.

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:23.035
-Your dad is permanently live
streaming himself,

00:03:23.035 --> 00:03:24.370
just so you've got two cameras,

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.872
so you can edit around him.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.872 --> 00:03:30.743
-Now we also talked, you know,
people like us, hosts,

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:34.180
we get asked -- it makes
natural sense to people,

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:35.915
"Oh, you should host
my charity event."

00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:37.783
-Oh, yeah.
-And you, over the years,

00:03:37.783 --> 00:03:40.319
have just eliminated
multiple charities

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:42.021
where you're suitable for.

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:43.756
-Yeah, I talk a lot
of [bleep].

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:47.192
-Yeah.
-So helpfully for me that --

00:03:47.192 --> 00:03:49.195
Yeah, I think I --
-It's, like, narrowed the field.

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:51.764
-Exactly. There's not many --
and so there's not many

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:55.501
that I can do
or that I'm welcome at now.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:57.069
-But then you mentioned the one
thing.

00:03:57.069 --> 00:03:58.337
-I mentioned last time.

00:03:58.337 --> 00:03:59.905
"Sesame Street's" the greatest.
I love --

00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:01.107
-Of course, the best.

00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:02.542
-Obviously, I love them
in general so much.

00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:04.577
I love the idea of them getting
as much money as they can.

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:07.480
So every year,
I do the "Sesame Street" gala.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.515
So I mentioned it here
because it's so fun,

00:04:09.515 --> 00:04:11.985
and I thought
this would be nice, too,

00:04:11.985 --> 00:04:16.589
and I knew it was coming up.
I was then booed at that gala.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:20.659
And I want you to think
about how hard it is

00:04:20.659 --> 00:04:21.894
to make that happen.

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:24.897
The theme of the evening
was kindness.

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:27.132
[ Laughter ]

00:04:27.132 --> 00:04:30.169
That's not easy to do.
Just to explain what happened --

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:32.705
So obviously, the whole point is
raise as much money as they can.

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:35.140
They got a big sponsor.
They had Bank of America

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:36.609
were going to be their sponsor,

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:37.844
and I think they got
Bank of America

00:04:37.844 --> 00:04:39.244
by making up this nonsense award

00:04:39.244 --> 00:04:40.780
they were going to give
to one of the executives.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:45.084
I was going to go on after her.
And she walked on and she said,

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:47.520
"I've been thinking about
how appropriate it is

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.356
that Bank of America and "Sesame
Street" have teamed up tonight,

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:53.892
because we are two organizations
built on kindness."

00:04:53.892 --> 00:04:57.396
And I was standing
at the side of the stage.

00:04:57.396 --> 00:05:00.365
I remember thinking,
"Hm.

00:05:00.365 --> 00:05:04.437
I'm going to let that go,
because no one needs me

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:07.540
to create a scene."
Alright now, you know,

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:08.808
she's out.
All the characters

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:10.309
are out there with her.

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:11.777
The whole point
is that we raise money.

00:05:11.777 --> 00:05:14.714
No one needs an incident
just because she said something

00:05:14.714 --> 00:05:18.550
that I personally find
a little odd to say,

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:21.187
so I can let that go.
I will choose to let that go.

00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:22.722
I'm going to make
the better choice right now.

00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:25.992
Then she said it three more
times in the next five minutes,

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:28.728
and I snapped and I walked out
and said,

00:05:28.728 --> 00:05:31.530
"It's been odd to hear talk of
kindness from Bank of America,

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.365
an institution that had
the highest foreclosure rates

00:05:33.365 --> 00:05:35.368
over the last ten years."

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:37.503
Boos bouncing --

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:42.240
Oh, that's the opposite
of the reaction in that room.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.676
And the worst thing, the thing
that has imprinted itself

00:05:44.676 --> 00:05:49.482
on my memory, is all I remember
is Bert looking up at me

00:05:49.482 --> 00:05:52.518
with such disappointment
in his eyes.

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:55.454
Just, "Why are you like this?

00:05:55.454 --> 00:06:00.526
You're behaving erratically,
and I live with Ernie."

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:02.962
It wasn't great, so I would
like to do it again.

00:06:02.962 --> 00:06:06.532
"Sesame Street," have me back.
I'll be a good boy this time.

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:08.234
-Yeah.

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:09.401
They can trust you on that.

00:06:09.401 --> 00:06:12.571
-Oh, definitely.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:12.571 --> 00:06:15.508
Just have McKinsey be a sponsor.
They'll be fine.

00:06:15.508 --> 00:06:16.709
-I've got a lot more
to ask you.

00:06:16.709 --> 00:06:18.878
We'll be right back with more
from John Oliver.

